"Normal thermal variation — busbars at different positions always run at different temperatures due to natural convection.\nAbnormal heating at the Phase B connection — likely caused by a loose busbar joint, corroded contact surface, or undersized contact area; the joint must be de-energised, the connection inspected, torqued, and re-tested by thermal imaging after reconnection.\nThe Phase B busbar has higher resistance because it carries more current — redistribute loads.\nNormal — Phase B (centre phase) runs hotter due to mutual heating from adjacent phases.",
