(D,"mcq",
"11. During inspection of a cable tray installation, the electrical inspector observes that instrumentation signal cables (0–10V analogue) and power cables (415V, 50 Hz) are installed in the same cable tray without any physical separation. What is the concern?",
"No concern — cable trays are designed for mixed voltage cables.\nElectromagnetic interference (EMI) — the 415V power cables radiate alternating magnetic fields that will induce noise voltages in the signal cables (especially analogue 0–10V), causing measurement errors; SAES-P-104 and IEC 61936 require physical separation (typically minimum 300 mm) or separate trays with earthed barriers between power and signal cables.\nMinor concern — ensure signal cables are shielded; shielding eliminates all EMI from power cables.\nNo concern — power and signal cables can share the same tray if they are on opposite sides.",
"Electromagnetic interference (EMI) — the 415V power cables radiate alternating magnetic fields that will induce noise voltages in the signal cables (especially analogue 0–10V), causing measurement errors; SAES-P-104 and IEC 61936 require physical separation (typically minimum 300 mm) or separate trays with earthed barriers between power and signal cables.",
"Power cables carrying alternating current create magnetic fields that couple into adjacent signal cables by inductive and capacitive mechanisms, creating noise on analogue and digital signals. The minimum separation requirements (typically ≥300 mm for unscreened cables, ≥150 mm with earthed separator) per SAES-P-104 and IEC standards must be maintained. Cable screening reduces but does not eliminate all EMI.",
1),

