(D,"mcq",
"15. A power factor correction capacitor bank (11 kV, 5 MVAR) is inspected. The capacitor bank has been in service for 3 years and the QC Inspector is reviewing the maintenance records. The capacitor bank nameplate states a maximum continuous operating voltage of 11.5 kV. The system voltage at the bus where the bank is connected is 11.9 kV (7.6% above nominal). What is the concern?",
"No concern — 11.9 kV is close to the nameplate maximum of 11.5 kV and the difference is within ±10% system voltage regulation tolerance.\nConcern — 11.9 kV exceeds the capacitor bank maximum continuous operating voltage of 11.5 kV; sustained overvoltage across capacitor cells accelerates insulation degradation and reduces capacitor life significantly; the system voltage must be regulated within the bank's rating or the bank temporarily switched out.\nNo concern — PFC capacitor banks are designed to operate continuously above rated voltage without derating.\nAccept — capacitor nameplate voltage is the nominal design voltage; ±10% is always acceptable.",
"Concern — 11.9 kV exceeds the capacitor bank maximum continuous operating voltage of 11.5 kV; sustained overvoltage across capacitor cells accelerates insulation degradation and reduces capacitor life significantly; the system voltage must be regulated within the bank's rating or the bank temporarily switched out.",
"Capacitor bank maximum continuous operating voltage is a limit — not a guideline. Sustained overvoltage causes increased dielectric stress across the capacitor dielectric (kV/mm), accelerating ageing exponentially. Capacitor life is inversely proportional to the voltage stress. Operating at 11.9 kV when the maximum is 11.5 kV represents a 3.5% overvoltage — seemingly small but significant for long-term reliability.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"16. During testing of a 415V distribution board, the measured prospective short-circuit current (PSCC) at the incoming terminals is 50 kA. The distribution board's rated short-circuit withstand capacity (Icw) is 25 kA. What is the finding?",
"Accept — the 50 kA fault level rarely occurs in practice; the 25 kA rating is sufficient for normal operating conditions.\nNCR — the prospective short circuit current (50 kA) exceeds the switchboard rated short circuit withstand capacity (25 kA); the switchboard is under-rated for this fault level; a switchboard with Icw ≥ 50 kA must be installed.\nAccept — install appropriate upstream fuses to limit the available fault current to 25 kA before the distribution board.\nAccept — the 50 kA PSCC at the incoming terminals does not mean the board will see 50 kA internally.",
"NCR — the prospective short circuit current (50 kA) exceeds the switchboard rated short circuit withstand capacity (25 kA); the switchboard is under-rated for this fault level; a switchboard with Icw ≥ 50 kA must be installed.",
"IEC 61439: switchgear and controlgear assemblies must be rated for the prospective fault current at their installation point. A switchboard with Icw 25 kA in a 50 kA fault current environment will catastrophically fail during a fault event — the busbars, current-carrying parts, and enclosure are not designed to withstand the electromechanical forces and thermal energy of a 50 kA fault. Either the switchboard must be upgraded or upstream current limitation must be formally engineered and verified.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"17. An electrical QC Inspector is reviewing the protection scheme for a 6.6 kV motor (750 kW). The protection relay settings include: 50/51 (overcurrent), 49 (thermal overload), and 87 (differential). For which of the following faults does the 87 (differential) protection provide the most reliable and fastest detection?",
"Ground faults on the motor terminal leads external to the motor winding.\nInter-turn short circuit within the motor stator winding (turn-to-turn fault within a single phase winding).\nOverload condition where the motor draws 120% of rated current.\nPhase-to-phase faults within the motor winding (internal phase-to-phase fault).",
"Phase-to-phase faults within the motor winding (internal phase-to-phase fault).",
"Differential (87) protection detects any current imbalance between the measured currents at the two ends of the protected zone (motor terminals). For large internal winding faults (phase-to-phase, phase-to-earth within the winding), the differential current is high and the 87 trips immediately. Inter-turn faults produce very small differential current and are poorly detected by standard 87 protection. Overload does not produce differential current — that is detected by 49.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"18. A 415V switchboard busbar inspection shows that the busbar material is painted aluminium. The joint between two busbar sections shows signs of white powder oxidation at the joint face. What is the finding?",
"Accept — aluminium naturally oxidises; the white oxide is a normal protective layer.\nNCR — aluminium busbars must have joint surfaces that are either silver-plated, tin-plated, or treated with a joint compound (anti-oxide grease) before bolted connection; aluminium oxide has very high electrical resistance and the joint will overheat in service; the joint must be remade with proper surface treatment.\nAccept — white oxidation on painted surfaces is a cosmetic issue only; the electrical connection is made through the bolted joint faces, not the painted surfaces.\nAccept — apply additional anti-corrosion spray to the joint and continue.",
"NCR — aluminium busbars must have joint surfaces that are either silver-plated, tin-plated, or treated with a joint compound (anti-oxide grease) before bolted connection; aluminium oxide has very high electrical resistance and the joint will overheat in service; the joint must be remade with proper surface treatment.",
"Aluminium naturally forms a thin, hard aluminium oxide (Al₂O₃) layer that has high electrical resistivity (~10¹⁴ Ω·m). At a bolted busbar joint, this oxide layer at the contact faces creates high contact resistance and severe heating. Aluminium-to-aluminium electrical joints must have the oxide layer mechanically removed and joint compound applied immediately before bolting, or joints plated (silver, tin) to maintain low contact resistance.",
1),

