"22. A new 11 kV/415V distribution transformer has just been installed. Before energisation, the inspector verifies the transformer ratio using a TTR (Transformer Turn Ratio) test. The nameplate turns ratio is 26.7:1. The TTR test result shows 27.3:1. What is the assessment?",
"Accept — the 2.2% difference from the nameplate is within the ±0.5% tolerance.\nNCR — the TTR result of 27.3:1 is 2.2% above the nameplate ratio of 26.7:1 (> ±0.5% tolerance per IEEE C57.12.91); the transformer may have a tap changer position error or an internal winding defect; verify tap changer position and re-test.\nAccept — TTR measurements have ±5% accuracy; 27.3:1 is within the instrument tolerance.\nAccept — transformer turns ratio is only important for synchronisation, not for general distribution service.",
"NCR — the TTR result of 27.3:1 is 2.2% above the nameplate ratio of 26.7:1 (> ±0.5% tolerance per IEEE C57.12.91); the transformer may have a tap changer position error or an internal winding defect; verify tap changer position and re-test.",
"IEEE C57.12.91: transformer turns ratio should be within ±0.5% of the nameplate ratio for each voltage tap. A 2.2% deviation indicates either the tap changer is on the wrong position (not at the correct tap for the tested ratio) or an internal winding issue. The inspector must first verify the tap changer position matches the test setup before escalating to a potential winding defect.",
