(D,"mcq",
"23. An electrical inspector reviews a cable schedule and notes that an 8-core armoured cable (SWA) has been used to supply a 3-phase motor (3 conductors + 1 earth). The remaining 4 cores are unused and left open at both ends. What is the concern?",
"No concern — unused cores are common in multi-core cables and cause no problems.\nConcern — the unused (floating) cores of an SWA cable, running parallel to the energised cores in the same cable, will have voltages induced in them by capacitive and inductive coupling from the energised conductors; these induced voltages can create a shock hazard if the spare cores are touched; spare cores should be earthed at one end to prevent floating potential buildup.\nConcern — unused cores increase the cable weight unnecessarily, reducing the cable tray load capacity.\nNo concern — the steel wire armour (SWA) shields the unused cores from inductive coupling.",
"Concern — the unused (floating) cores of an SWA cable, running parallel to the energised cores in the same cable, will have voltages induced in them by capacitive and inductive coupling from the energised conductors; these induced voltages can create a shock hazard if the spare cores are touched; spare cores should be earthed at one end to prevent floating potential buildup.",
"Unused cores in a power cable that carry current in adjacent cores will have capacitively and inductively coupled voltages induced into them. These floating cores can reach dangerous touch voltages (tens to hundreds of volts) even though no current flows in them. Standard practice: earth all unused spare cores at one end (typically the supply end) to prevent floating potential. This is a safety wiring requirement per IEE Wiring Regulations and SAES-P-104.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"24. An electrical inspection of a control panel reveals that wire identification labels have been installed on the main 415V power cables but not on the individual control wires within the panel. The project specification requires labelling per IEC 60445 at both ends of every wire. What is the finding?",
"Accept — control wire labelling is a contractor's internal housekeeping standard, not a code requirement.\nNCR — IEC 60445 requires all electrical conductors to be identified at both termination points; missing control wire labels make maintenance, troubleshooting, and fault-finding significantly more difficult and error-prone, increasing risk of incorrect disconnections; all control wires must be labelled before commissioning.\nAccept — control wires within a panel can be identified by colour code (phase colours); labels are only required externally.\nAccept — control wire labelling is only required for cables leaving the panel.",
"NCR — IEC 60445 requires all electrical conductors to be identified at both termination points; missing control wire labels make maintenance, troubleshooting, and fault-finding significantly more difficult and error-prone, increasing risk of incorrect disconnections; all control wires must be labelled before commissioning.",
"IEC 60445 and standard electrical practice require all wires/conductors to be identified at both terminations. Unlabelled control wires in panels are a significant safety and maintenance issue — incorrect disconnection during maintenance can cause control system faults, equipment damage, or injury. Labelling is a commissioning prerequisite, not optional.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"25. A 415V, 50 Hz power supply is connected to a 60 Hz motor in an emergency supply situation. The motor is rated 415V, 60 Hz, 100 kW. Operating at 50 Hz instead of 60 Hz, what is the most significant impact on the motor?",
"The motor will run at a higher speed due to lower frequency — approximately 120% of rated speed.\nThe motor will run at a lower speed (50/60 = 83.3% of rated speed) and the flux density will increase by the same ratio (415V/50Hz vs. rated 415V/60Hz), causing magnetic saturation, increased core losses, higher temperature, and possible insulation failure.\nThe motor will consume less power at 50 Hz and will be cooler than at 60 Hz.\nThe motor will not start because the frequency mismatch prevents synchronisation.",
"The motor will run at a lower speed (50/60 = 83.3% of rated speed) and the flux density will increase by the same ratio (415V/50Hz vs. rated 415V/60Hz), causing magnetic saturation, increased core losses, higher temperature, and possible insulation failure.",
"Induction motor speed is proportional to frequency (N = 120f/p). At 50 Hz vs. 60 Hz (same voltage), speed decreases to 83.3% of rated. More critically, at the same voltage but lower frequency, V/f ratio increases — magnetic flux density (Φ = V/(4.44 × f × N)) increases, causing core saturation, significantly higher core losses, and overheating. This is why VFDs must maintain constant V/f ratio when reducing frequency.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"26. A motor's insulation class is H (maximum continuous operating temperature 180°C, per IEC 60085). The measured winding temperature rise at full load (by resistance method) is 95°C, and the ambient temperature is 40°C. What is the total winding temperature and is it within the class H limit?",
"Total winding temperature = 95 + 40 = 135°C; within class H limit (180°C) — acceptable.\nTotal winding temperature = 95°C (rise only); within class H limit (180°C) — acceptable.\nTotal winding temperature = 95 + 40 + 10 (hot spot correction) = 145°C; within class H limit (180°C) — acceptable.\nTotal winding temperature = 95 + 40 = 135°C; exceeds class F limit (155°C) but within class H — borderline, recommend investigation.",
"Total winding temperature = 95 + 40 + 10 (hot spot correction) = 145°C; within class H limit (180°C) — acceptable.",
"IEC 60034-1 winding temperature assessment: total temperature = ambient temperature + measured temperature rise (resistance method) + hot spot correction factor (10°C per IEC 60034-1). Total = 40 + 95 + 10 = 145°C. Class H limit = 180°C. 145°C < 180°C → acceptable. The 10°C hot spot correction accounts for the difference between the average winding temperature (measured by resistance) and the hottest point in the winding.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"27. During a loop check of a motor start circuit, the QC Inspector finds that the motor thermistor (PTC type) is connected to the relay but the relay parameter is set for NTC (Negative Temperature Coefficient). Will the protection function correctly?",
"Yes — PTC and NTC thermistors provide equivalent protection; the relay setting type doesn't matter.\nNo — a PTC thermistor increases resistance with temperature; an NTC thermistor decreases resistance with temperature; if the relay is set for NTC but connected to a PTC, the relay will interpret the increasing resistance (as the motor heats up) as a DECREASING temperature signal and will NOT trip on overtemperature — the protection is inverted and will fail to protect the motor.\nNo — PTC thermistors cannot be used with motor protection relays.\nYes — the relay automatically detects whether the connected thermistor is PTC or NTC.",
"No — a PTC thermistor increases resistance with temperature; an NTC thermistor decreases resistance with temperature; if the relay is set for NTC but connected to a PTC, the relay will interpret the increasing resistance (as the motor heats up) as a DECREASING temperature signal and will NOT trip on overtemperature — the protection is inverted and will fail to protect the motor.",
"PTC (Positive Temperature Coefficient) thermistors show very high resistance increase at the trip temperature — used widely in motor windings for thermal protection (Klixon/PTC type). NTC thermistors decrease in resistance with temperature — often used in temperature measurement applications. If the relay is incorrectly configured for the wrong thermistor type, the protection logic is inverted — the relay will not trip at overtemperature conditions. This is a critical commissioning error requiring correction.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"28. An electrical QC inspector is reviewing HVAC room condition records for a 415V main switchroom. The humidity in the switchroom is found to be 85% RH continuously. The switchgear is rated for maximum 95% RH non-condensing. What is the concern?",
"No concern — 85% RH is below the 95% RH maximum rating; the equipment is within specification.\nConcern — sustained 85% RH in a switchroom is near the condensation threshold; even non-condensing humidity specifications assume adequate ventilation and temperature stability; at 85% RH, small temperature drops can cause condensation on insulating surfaces, leading to tracking and flashover; the HVAC system must be reviewed and anti-condensation heaters must be verified as operational.\nNo concern — switchgear IP ratings prevent moisture from affecting internal components.\nAccept — 85% RH is within the 95% RH limit; no action required.",
"Concern — sustained 85% RH in a switchroom is near the condensation threshold; even non-condensing humidity specifications assume adequate ventilation and temperature stability; at 85% RH, small temperature drops can cause condensation on insulating surfaces, leading to tracking and flashover; the HVAC system must be reviewed and anti-condensation heaters must be verified as operational.",
"Switchgear in high-humidity environments is at risk for condensation during temperature transients (e.g., HVAC shutdown overnight, cool mornings). Condensation on HV insulating surfaces leads to tracking currents and potentially flashover. 85% RH requires careful HVAC management and functional anti-condensation heaters in the switchgear. Many standards recommend maintaining <60% RH in switchrooms as best practice.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"29. During commissioning of a 415V motor control panel, the QC Inspector performs an earth continuity test from the motor frame to the substation MV/LV transformer earth bar. The measured earth loop resistance is 1.8 Ω. The maximum allowable earth fault loop resistance for the protection relay to operate within 0.4 seconds per IEC 60364-4-41 is 0.5 Ω. What is the finding?",
"Accept — 1.8 Ω is below the general 5 Ω maximum earth resistance required by IEE wiring regulations.\nNCR — at 1.8 Ω earth loop resistance, the fault current during a ground fault would be too low (I = V/Z = 240V/1.8Ω = 133 A) for the overcurrent protective device to trip within 0.4 seconds for a 240V to earth fault; the earth continuity must be improved (lower resistance earth loop) to ensure rapid disconnection on earth fault.\nAccept — earth loop resistance is not a commissioning test parameter for motor panels.\nAccept — the motor frame is connected to the panel earth bar; continuity to the substation is the civil/earthing contractor's responsibility.",
"NCR — at 1.8 Ω earth loop resistance, the fault current during a ground fault would be too low (I = V/Z = 240V/1.8Ω = 133 A) for the overcurrent protective device to trip within 0.4 seconds for a 240V to earth fault; the earth continuity must be improved (lower resistance earth loop) to ensure rapid disconnection on earth fault.",
"IEC 60364-4-41: ADS (Automatic Disconnection of Supply) requires the earth fault current to be sufficient to operate the protective device within the specified disconnection time. High earth loop resistance limits the fault current, causing slow or no tripping, leaving personnel exposed to dangerous touch voltages for extended periods. The earth loop must be sized to ensure adequate fault current flow.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"30. An electrical QC audit finds that the project uses Class II (double-insulated) power tools on a construction site with no earth (ground) connection. Workers are using these tools outdoors in the Arabian Gulf summer heat, with wet hands from perspiration. What is the correct safety assessment?",
"Acceptable — Class II double-insulated tools require no earth connection and provide equivalent protection.\nClass II tools are appropriate for removing earth dependency; however, the wet hand and outdoor conditions should trigger additional precaution — GFCI/RCD protection should be required for all portable tools regardless of insulation class per SAES-T-003 and OSHA electrical safety requirements.\nNot acceptable — Class II tools are not permitted on any Saudi Aramco site.\nNot acceptable — all power tools used in the Arabian Gulf must have Class I (earthed) construction with double insulation as well.",
"Class II tools are appropriate for removing earth dependency; however, the wet hand and outdoor conditions should trigger additional precaution — GFCI/RCD protection should be required for all portable tools regardless of insulation class per SAES-T-003 and OSHA electrical safety requirements.",
"Class II tools eliminate the earth fault path through the tool body — providing excellent basic protection. However, GFCI/RCD protection is a defence-in-depth measure that catches residual leakage current from all types of equipment, including Class II (insulation failures can still occur). SAES-T-003 construction safety requirements mandate GFCI protection for construction-site portable tools — this applies even to Class II tools in high-risk environments (wet conditions, outdoor, elevated temperature).",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"31. A 6.6 kV synchronous motor (2,500 kW) is being commissioned. During the synchronisation sequence, the operator closes the breaker at an incorrect rotor angle, causing a violent synchronising shock. What measurement on the synchronoscope should be verified before breaker closure?",
"Voltage magnitude should exactly match on both sides of the breaker before closure.\nThe synchronoscope pointer should be rotating slowly clockwise and approaching the 12 o'clock position (matching conditions: frequency, voltage magnitude, and phase angle close to zero) before breaker closure.\nThe power factor angle between the incoming machine and the bus should be 90° for optimal synchronisation.\nThe speed of the incoming machine should be exactly 5% below synchronous speed for soft synchronisation.",
"The synchronoscope pointer should be rotating slowly clockwise and approaching the 12 o'clock position (matching conditions: frequency, voltage magnitude, and phase angle close to zero) before breaker closure.",
"Synchronoscope: the pointer rotates at a speed proportional to the frequency difference between the incoming machine and the busbar. At the 12 o'clock position, phase angle difference is zero — the two systems are in phase. The breaker must be closed just before the pointer reaches 12 o'clock (allowing for breaker operating time) to minimise synchronising shock. Closing out of phase causes potentially destructive mechanical and electrical transients.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"32. An ARAMCO electrical QC inspector finds that the project electrical engineer has installed a neutral earthing resistor (NER) rated 400 A continuous on the secondary of an 11 kV/415V transformer. The transformer is 1,000 kVA. The full load current on the 415V side is 1,390 A. What is the concern?",
"No concern — a 400 A NER limits the earth fault current, which is a standard design approach.\nMajor design error — a 400 A continuous-rated NER on a 415V system where the transformer full-load current is 1,390 A will significantly limit the earth fault current to 400 A; this may be intentional (high-resistance earthing) but must be verified against the protection relay settings — earth fault protection relays must be calibrated to detect fault currents as low as 400 A; additionally, 400 A continuous is unusual for a 415V NER — this may be an HV NER specification applied incorrectly.\nNo concern — NER rating does not affect the protection coordination.\nAccept — the NER provides a fault-limiting function and is a common design for 415V systems.",
"Major design error — a 400 A continuous-rated NER on a 415V system where the transformer full-load current is 1,390 A will significantly limit the earth fault current to 400 A; this may be intentional (high-resistance earthing) but must be verified against the protection relay settings — earth fault protection relays must be calibrated to detect fault currents as low as 400 A; additionally, 400 A continuous is unusual for a 415V NER — this may be an HV NER specification applied incorrectly.",
"NER (Neutral Earthing Resistor) design must be matched to the system fault current requirements and protection relay sensitivity. A 400 A NER limiting earth fault current to 400 A on a 1,390 A FLC system is either an intentional high-resistance earthing design (with appropriate protection settings) or a design error where an HV-system NER specification was incorrectly applied to the LV system. The protection engineer must verify the design intent and relay settings.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"33. An electrical QC Inspector witnesses a HiPot (high-potential dielectric withstand) test on a new 6.6 kV cable. The test voltage applied is 20 kV DC for 15 minutes. During the test, the leakage current starts at 2 μA and increases to 45 μA over the 15-minute period. What does this trend indicate?",
"Normal behaviour — leakage current always increases during a DC HiPot test due to absorption current effects.\nConcern — a rapidly increasing leakage current (2 μA to 45 μA) during a HiPot test indicates a defective or degraded insulation; good insulation shows a decreasing leakage current over time (absorption current diminishes); increasing leakage current indicates a conduction path developing through the insulation; the test should be stopped to prevent insulation failure and the cable should be investigated.\nAcceptable — the leakage current must be compared only to the maximum allowable value, not the trend.\nNormal — the increase is due to temperature rise of the cable during the test increasing conductivity.",
"Concern — a rapidly increasing leakage current (2 μA to 45 μA) during a DC HiPot test indicates a defective or degraded insulation; good insulation shows a decreasing leakage current over time (absorption current diminishes); increasing leakage current indicates a conduction path developing through the insulation; the test should be stopped to prevent insulation failure and the cable should be investigated.",
"During a DC HiPot test, leakage current through good insulation follows three phases: initial transient (capacitive charging, decays quickly), absorption (polarisation, decays slowly), and steady conduction (constant low value). Increasing leakage current indicates either insulation breakdown is in progress or a conduction path (moisture, contamination, void breakdown) is developing. The test should be stopped — continuing risks completing the insulation failure.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"34. A motor rated 415V, 50 Hz is connected to a VFD (Variable Frequency Drive). The VFD output voltage waveform is a pulse-width modulated (PWM) signal. During commissioning, the inspector measures the motor terminal voltage using a standard Fluke 87V true-RMS multimeter. The reading shows 415V. The VFD manufacturer specifies a maximum voltage at the motor terminals of 800V peak for the motor winding insulation. What additional measurement is needed?",
"No additional measurement — if the RMS voltage is correct, the peak voltage is correct.\nA peak voltage measurement or motor terminal overvoltage assessment is needed — PWM waveforms have high-frequency voltage spikes (due to cable reflections and dv/dt switching transients) that can significantly exceed the nominal RMS voltage; a standard RMS meter does not capture these peaks; a high-speed oscilloscope or VFD-specific voltage probe is needed to measure actual peak voltage at the motor terminals, especially for long motor cable runs.\nMeasure the current with a clamp meter — high current indicates high peak voltage.\nNo additional measurement — 415V is within the motor rating.",
"A peak voltage measurement or motor terminal overvoltage assessment is needed — PWM waveforms have high-frequency voltage spikes (due to cable reflections and dv/dt switching transients) that can significantly exceed the nominal RMS voltage; a standard RMS meter does not capture these peaks; a high-speed oscilloscope or VFD-specific voltage probe is needed to measure actual peak voltage at the motor terminals, especially for long motor cable runs.",
"VFD PWM output voltage contains high-frequency spikes from cable-end reflections (Bergeron transmission line reflections) that can significantly exceed the DC bus voltage (typically >1,000 V peaks). Standard RMS meters do not capture these microsecond-duration spikes. Over long cable runs without dv/dt filters or output reactors, these spikes can damage standard motor winding insulation. Peak voltage measurement or calculation per IEC 61800-5-2 is required.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"35. An ARAMCO electrical inspector is checking the isolation coordination for a new 415V switchboard. The downstream MCBs are rated Icn = 10 kA and the upstream moulded case circuit breaker (MCCB) is rated Ics = 36 kA. The prospective fault current at the MCB terminals is 18 kA. The MCB manufacturer's specification shows that with a suitable backup fuse, the let-through energy (I²t) at 18 kA fault is acceptable. Is the installation acceptable?",
"No — the MCBs must be independently rated for the full 18 kA prospective fault current without backup protection.\nYes — if the upstream MCCB or fuse provides discrimination and limits the peak let-through current to within the MCB Icn rating, this represents a 'back-up protection' arrangement per IEC 60947-2, which is acceptable when verified by test evidence from the manufacturer.\nNo — MCBs with Icn < prospective fault current are never acceptable regardless of backup protection.\nYes — the MCB Ics rating of 10 kA is always measured with backup protection; 18 kA is acceptable.",
"Yes — if the upstream MCCB or fuse provides discrimination and limits the peak let-through current to within the MCB Icn rating, this represents a 'back-up protection' arrangement per IEC 60947-2, which is acceptable when verified by test evidence from the manufacturer.",
"IEC 60947-2 allows 'back-up protection' where a downstream device (MCB) that has insufficient independent fault current rating is protected by an upstream device (MCCB or fuse) that limits the let-through energy/current to within the MCB's capability. This must be verified by manufacturer test evidence (table or certificate) showing the specific upstream/downstream device combination. Not all combinations are valid — only those tested together.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"36. A 415V cable tray inspection shows armoured cables (SWA) with the armour bonded and earthed at both cable ends. A cable tray that is not earthed directly is used. An unarmoured cable (without SWA) is also installed in the same tray. What is the concern regarding the unarmoured cable?",
"No concern — unarmoured cables do not require earthing if they are installed inside a cable tray.\nConcern — the unarmoured cable has no inherent sheath protection or earth screening; if a fault occurs and the cable tray is not earthed, the tray becomes a floating metallic part that can rise to a dangerous touch potential; additionally, the unarmoured cable lacks mechanical protection against physical damage; cable tray must be earthed and armoured cables (or equivalent) should be used in this environment.\nNo concern — the armoured cables' earth bond provides earth reference for the entire tray.\nNo concern — SAES-P-104 does not require cable tray earthing.",
"Concern — the unarmoured cable has no inherent sheath protection or earth screening; if a fault occurs and the cable tray is not earthed, the tray becomes a floating metallic part that can rise to a dangerous touch potential; additionally, the unarmoured cable lacks mechanical protection against physical damage; cable tray must be earthed and armoured cables (or equivalent) should be used in this environment.",
"Cable trays are metallic structures that must be bonded and earthed per IEE Wiring Regulations and SAES-P-104. An unearthed metallic tray carrying a cable with a fault can rise to a dangerous touch potential. Additionally, unarmoured cables in areas requiring mechanical protection (outdoor, industrial) should use armoured cables per the project specification.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"37. During a SAT (Site Acceptance Test) for a 132 kV GIS (Gas-Insulated Switchgear), the inspector observes that the SF6 gas pressure in one section shows 0.42 MPa (absolute) when the required pressure is 0.52 MPa (absolute) at 20°C. What is the implication of this SF6 pressure deficiency?",
"The GIS section can operate at reduced insulating capability at the lower SF6 pressure.\nThe GIS section must NOT be energised — SF6 insulation strength is directly proportional to gas density; at 0.42 MPa instead of 0.52 MPa, the dielectric strength is significantly reduced and the insulation coordination (designed for rated pressure) is invalid; the gas must be topped up to rated pressure and verified before any HV energisation.\nAccept — 0.42 MPa is above the atmospheric pressure (0.1 MPa) so there is still meaningful SF6 insulation present.\nAccept — reduce the operating voltage in proportion to the reduced gas pressure.",
"The GIS section must NOT be energised — SF6 insulation strength is directly proportional to gas density; at 0.42 MPa instead of 0.52 MPa, the dielectric strength is significantly reduced and the insulation coordination (designed for rated pressure) is invalid; the gas must be topped up to rated pressure and verified before any HV energisation.",
"SF6 GIS: the insulation system is designed for a specific SF6 gas density (or pressure at reference temperature). The dielectric strength of SF6 is a function of gas density. At 0.42/0.52 = 81% of rated pressure, the dielectric strength is significantly below the design value — the switchgear will not meet its insulation level. Energising the GIS at reduced SF6 pressure risks flashover. The gas density (pressure and temperature) must be verified and corrected before energisation.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"38. An electrical inspector reviews the cable schedule for a new installation and finds that a 50 mm² Cu cable is being used for a 100 kW, 415V, 3-phase motor supply over a cable run of 150 m. Calculate the approximate voltage drop (using the formula V_drop = √3 × I × L × R per metre of conductor, where R = 0.387 mΩ/m for 50 mm² Cu) and state whether it meets the 5% maximum voltage drop criterion.",
"Full load current I = 100,000 / (√3 × 415 × 0.85 PF) ≈ 164 A; V_drop = √3 × 164 × 150 × 0.000387 ≈ 65.6V; as % of 415V = 15.8% — exceeds 5% — cable is undersized.\nFull load current I = 100,000 / (√3 × 415) ≈ 139 A; V_drop = 139 × 150 × 0.000387 ≈ 8.1V; 8.1/415 = 2% — within 5% — acceptable.\nFull load current I ≈ 164 A; V_drop = 164 × 150 × 0.000387 ≈ 9.5V; 9.5/415 = 2.3% — within 5% — acceptable.\nFull load current ≈ 139 A; V_drop = √3 × 139 × 150 × 0.000387 ≈ 14V; 14/415 = 3.4% — within 5% — acceptable.",
"Full load current ≈ 139 A; V_drop = √3 × 139 × 150 × 0.000387 ≈ 14V; 14/415 = 3.4% — within 5% — acceptable.",
"3-phase voltage drop = √3 × I × L × R_per_metre. FLC = P / (√3 × V × PF) = 100,000 / (√3 × 415 × 0.85) ≈ 164 A (with 0.85 PF) OR 100,000 / (√3 × 415) ≈ 139 A (unity PF, simplified). Using simplified: V_drop = √3 × 139 × 0.150 km × 0.387 Ω/km = 1.732 × 139 × 0.150 × 0.387 = 1.732 × 8.07 = 14.0 V. 14/415 = 3.4% < 5% — ACCEPTABLE. The key is applying √3 for 3-phase voltage drop calculation correctly.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"39. An electrical inspector observes that a welder's power return cable (earth clamp) is clamped to a pipeline 10 metres from the weld location. The weld is on a section of pipe adjacent to an instrumentation signal cable that runs parallel to the pipe. What is the concern?",
"No concern — the return cable position does not affect the weld quality or instrumentation.\nStray welding current concern — with the earth clamp far from the weld, welding return current may travel through the pipe wall and other metallic paths (including instrument cable shields or cable trays) rather than directly through the return cable; this stray current can damage instrumentation, introduce measurement errors, and corrode metallic surfaces along the stray current path.\nNo concern — stray welding current only affects non-metallic components.\nMinor concern — place the earth clamp closer to the weld to reduce weld spatter.",
"Stray welding current concern — with the earth clamp far from the weld, welding return current may travel through the pipe wall and other metallic paths (including instrument cable shields or cable trays) rather than directly through the return cable; this stray current can damage instrumentation, introduce measurement errors, and corrode metallic surfaces along the stray current path.",
"Welding stray current control: the earth (work) return cable should be as close to the weld as practicable on the same component. A return clamp 10 m away from the weld on a metallic pipeline allows the welding return current to follow the lowest resistance path — which may include adjacent metallic structures, instrument cable shields, or pipe flanges. Stray welding currents can damage instruments, cause arc burns at instrumentation connections, and accelerate corrosion.",
1),

(D,"mcq",
"40. An electrical QC Inspector conducts a final inspection of a substation before energisation. He reviews the relay test records and finds that the differential protection (87T) for a 33/11 kV transformer has been tested and set correctly. However, there is no record of the through-fault stability test (checking that the 87T does NOT trip during a simulated external fault). What is the concern?",
"No concern — differential relays only need to be tested for tripping, not stability.\nCritical concern — without a through-fault stability test, the relay may trip incorrectly during external faults (spurious operation), causing unnecessary outages; the through-fault stability test verifies that the relay's restraint characteristic prevents operation for external faults where both CTs see the same current; the 87T must be verified for both trip sensitivity (internal faults) and stability (external faults) before commissioning.\nAccept — the relay manufacturer's default settings ensure through-fault stability without specific testing.\nAccept — through-fault stability testing is only required for generator differential protection (87G), not transformer differential (87T).",
"Critical concern — without a through-fault stability test, the relay may trip incorrectly during external faults (spurious operation), causing unnecessary outages; the through-fault stability test verifies that the relay's restraint characteristic prevents operation for external faults where both CTs see the same current; the 87T must be verified for both trip sensitivity (internal faults) and stability (external faults) before commissioning.",
"Differential protection commissioning must include both pickup (internal fault trip) AND stability (external fault no-trip) verification. Through-fault stability testing uses a secondary injection test to simulate the CT secondary currents during an external fault — if the relay trips in this condition, there is a wiring error (CT polarity, ratio mismatch) or incorrect setting. Commissioning without this test leaves a critical uncertainty in the protection scheme.",
1),

